Draft for discussion. This proposes adopting BIP352 silent payments, restricted to P2PKH inputs, as a reusable address format for Dogecoin. A working implementation exists and is linked below. BIP352 specifies the eligibility rule it depends on; what is measured here, over the whole chain rather than a sample, is that the rule never fails to find a key and never finds two. Two reviewers have built it and run the tests, and one wrote independent attacks against it; the cryptography itself has had no outside eyes.

== Abstract ==

A silent payment address is a reusable static string that a sender turns into an ordinary one-time P2PKH output, by performing ECDH against the address's scan key and the transaction's own inputs. A tip jar can publish one string forever and every payment to it still lands on a distinct address that no third party can link back to the published string.

This is wallet level. There is no consensus change, no fork, no new script type, and a chain that never adopts it is unaffected. The outputs are plain P2PKH and indistinguishable from any other payment. A BIP352 output on Bitcoin is an ordinary Taproot output and does not stand out among Taproot outputs either; what differs is the share of the chain each type covers, and P2PKH is a far larger share of Dogecoin than Taproot is of Bitcoin.

== Motivation ==

Dogecoin's culture is built on publishing one address and receiving from strangers. Tip jars, donation addresses, project wallets, addresses pasted into reddit threads in 2014 that are still receiving. That is address reuse, and it is the exact pattern silent payments fixes. Bitcoin mostly solved the same problem socially by pushing per-invoice addresses through payment processors, which Dogecoin never had and mostly does not want.

The measurement backs it up rather than just the intuition. Walking every block to height 6450053, 268927881 transactions have at least one input eligible under the rule as it now stands, out of 413471029 non-coinbase transactions. That is 65 percent of all Dogecoin transactions. An earlier draft gave 278571058 here as an upper bound, taken before the rule was narrowed to compressed keys; narrowing it removed 3.5 percent of the count.

== Scanning cost is the real constraint ==

A receiver cannot be told which outputs are theirs, so they derive a candidate for every transaction carrying an eligible input and check it. That is one ECDH per such transaction, forever, and it is the thing that decides whether this is usable rather than the cryptography.

The numbers are measured over the whole chain rather than estimated. 268927881 transactions have an eligible input, so the tweaks themselves are 33 bytes each, which is 8874620073 bytes of payload. Scanning the full history once is one <code>ecmult_const</code> per entry, so 268927881 of them, which at 50 microseconds a call is about 3.7 hours of single core CPU. After that, incremental scanning is one minute's worth of blocks at a time and costs nothing worth measuring.

The on-disk figure that stood here, 10771497975 bytes, is not restated, because the index has not been rebuilt under the current rule. The datadir the earlier figure came from has a block index whose recorded undo position for height 1111 is two bytes short of where that record actually starts, so a build from genesis stops there. The undo file itself is consistent and every record in it verifies against its own checksum, and the same read succeeds for the filter index built on the same datadir earlier, so this is one datadir's bookkeeping rather than a property of the rule or the index.

The node pays separately, though it pays the same amount. Building the index is also one curve multiplication per entry, so the same 3.7 hours, spread across a sync rather than paid in one sitting. The index syncs in its own thread and derives each tweak from block undo data, so enabling it on a node that is already synced catches up in the background rather than requiring a reindex. A regtest demo hides the build cost entirely, so it is stated here rather than discovered at deployment.

A full node carries that easily. A light client is harder but not excluded, and the distinction is birth height. The 8.9GB figure counts the tweaks alone. On the wire a <code>CTweakEntry</code> is a 4-byte index and a length-prefixed key, so 38 bytes, and a block's mean is 1584 bytes rather than 1376, before message framing. A wallet created today scans only forward, so roughly 2MB a day at the long-run mean, but the mean is the wrong statistic for a chain whose daily transaction count has ranged from tens of thousands to over two million. That is an ordinary mobile sync.

The client derives its candidate output scripts from the tweaks and then needs to know whether any appear in a block without downloading it, which is exactly what a BIP158 compact block filter answers. What that costs is measurable against the filters a mainnet node already serves. Over 400 blocks ending at height 6459495, 200000000 queries for scripts no receiver owns returned 245 matches, so one false positive in 816327 queries against the one in 784931 the parameters specify, which 245 events place it well inside. The control for that number is that 133 real output scripts taken from those same blocks were all found in their own filters, so the key derivation and the Golomb-Rice decoding are not quietly wrong in a way that would suppress matches and flatter the rate. At 41.7 candidates per block, the long-run mean, a scanner therefore downloads a block it did not need about once in 18800 blocks, or once per two weeks of chain at one minute a block.

What has not run against mainnet is the client itself. The measurement above exercises the filters and the parameters, not the <code>getsptweaks</code> round trip, because serving tweaks needs the index and the index does not build on the datadir available. So the filter arithmetic is measured and the end to end path is still regtest only.

A scan over history is only half of a restore. Finding an output the wallet can spend says nothing about whether it is still unspent, so a scan has to be followed by a search for what spent what it found; without that the wallet credits coins that are long gone and reports a balance it cannot spend. The linked tree does that search, and it is the second of the three money-losing defects the third review round found.

Restoring an old wallet is the case that stays expensive, since it means tweaks back to its birth height, up to the full history. The alternative is handing a server the scan key, which reveals every payment that wallet has ever received and gives up the point of the scheme. There is a third option this proposal does not take: BIP352's scanning rule lets a server skip transactions with no unspent output of the paying type, so a restore that wants the balance rather than the history needs only those tweaks. The count of transactions still holding an unspent P2PKH output is measurable from the chainstate and is the number that decision needs.

== What it covers and what it ignores ==

P2PKH inputs only, and compressed keys only. Of 771450807 inputs on the chain, 528444159 are eligible, 217606786 are not P2PKH at all, and 214220226 of those are P2SH. A further 25399862 are P2PKH spends revealing an uncompressed key, which BIP352 does not permit. So this ignores 31.5 percent of all inputs by design. A sender whose coins are all in P2SH cannot pay a silent payment address at all, and that is a real hole rather than a rounding error.

Of that P2SH, 80.4 percent of those inputs do reveal exactly one compressed public key as the first push of the redeem script, which looks at first like a large extension worth having, 22.3 percent of all inputs on the chain. Multisig is 10.0 percent and 9.5 percent reveals no key at all. Those three shares are whole-chain, and decoding what the scripts in the first of them actually do takes the number away again. That decoding was run over a sample rather than the whole chain, so the shares below are the sample's. 97 percent of them are a single key push followed by <code>OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY</code> and then a run of <code>OP_DROP</code>s, which is the reveal side of a data-carrying protocol, and the shape is not confined to the 2023 inscription wave. Files from well before it show the same 97 percent. The inputs carrying no key are the other half of the same traffic: a data push followed by <code>OP_2DROP OP_2DROP OP_2DROP OP_1</code>.

So the reachable P2SH is data carriers almost to the exclusion of anything else. Those inputs are not wallets holding coin that someone would spend on a payment, and extending the eligibility rule to cover them would buy close to nothing while adding rules about which redeem shapes commit to their key unconditionally. BIP352 excludes P2SH because a redeem script can branch and a participant can re-sign down a different path after the outputs were derived. The measurement says there is no demand on the other side of that risk worth taking it for. P2SH is out of scope, and the 22.3 percent ceiling is reported here only so nobody has to rediscover that it overstates real demand by a factor of about thirty.

== Evidence for the eligibility rule ==

Both sides have to agree on which pubkey a given input contributes, because the sender and the receiver each hash the same set of input pubkeys into the shared secret. Disagree and the payment is derivable by neither, so the coins land on an address nobody holds the key to. This is the part that loses funds if it is wrong.

The rule is to walk every data push in the P2PKH scriptSig and take the push whose HASH160 matches the prevout's pubkey hash. BIP352 requires exactly this, in those words: the receiver must parse the scriptSig for the public key even where it does not match the template, because P2PKH scriptSigs are malleable by third parties.

P2PKH here means the canonical 25 bytes and not whatever 1.14's <code>Solver</code> calls <code>TX_PUBKEYHASH</code>, which matches the template by opcode and so also accepts a 20-byte hash pushed with <code>OP_PUSHDATA1</code>, <code>2</code> or <code>4</code>. Spending such an output is non-standard and needs a miner to confirm it, so the reach is small either way, but the reader, the sender and the corpus tool have to agree on which inputs they are talking about, and the exact 25 bytes is what all three take. An input whose prevout pushes its hash with <code>OP_PUSHDATA1</code> is therefore left out, which a differential run against an independent implementation of this text confirms.

What is not in the spec is whether that parse can fail, and it cannot. <code>OP_CHECKSIG</code> consumes a 33 or 65 byte element, and no opcode left enabled produces one that long: the splice opcodes are disabled, the hash opcodes give 20 or 32 bytes, and arithmetic gives at most 5. So the key was pushed, and <code>OP_EQUALVERIFY</code> fails unless it hashes to the prevout. A no_match is therefore not a shape some future wallet can wander into, and the measurement below confirms the argument rather than standing in for it.

Every block to 6450053, read out of the blk and rev files directly:

<pre>
    inputs classified   771450807
    key recovered       553844021   all canonical
      compressed        528444159   eligible under this rule
      uncompressed       25399862   not eligible, see below
    no_match                    0
    ambiguous                   0
</pre>

The harness walked the pushes without the encoding filter, so the recovered count measures
whether the rule can find a key at all, which is the thing in question. Eligibility then takes
the compressed subset.

A no_match would be a P2PKH input where no push matches, meaning neither party can derive from it. An ambiguous would be two distinct pushes both matching, which needs a HASH160 collision. Both are zero. 528444159 of the recovered keys are compressed and 25399862 uncompressed, and every single one sits in the canonical two-push form, signature then pubkey, so the simpler rule that just takes the last push would have found all of them too. The general rule is kept anyway, because the cost of walking the pushes is nothing and the cost of being wrong once is somebody's coins. What the canonical share establishes is that no wallet on this chain has yet produced a shape the simpler rule would miss, not that none can: the shape is third-party malleable, so the measurement bounds what has happened rather than what is possible.

Coverage is the whole chain rather than a sample. 413471029 non-coinbase transactions were classified across 6450054 blocks and not one input was left unresolved, because the prevout script comes from the same undo record as the input rather than from a lookup that can miss. 93 block records in the files had no undo record to pair with, genesis among them by construction, and those are counted and skipped rather than guessed at.

Bitcoin Core's functional test framework has since grown an independent implementation of the same extraction, in <code>src/common/bip352.cpp</code>. Its P2PKH branch walks the scriptSig pushes and takes the one whose HASH160 matches the prevout, which is the rule above arrived at separately by someone else from the same spec. Run over the vectors here, the two agree on the input pubkey sum and the input hash in all 17 eligible cases.

They agree. Both take 33-byte compressed pushes only, which is what BIP352 permits.

An earlier version of this rule also accepted the 65-byte uncompressed form, and the reasoning for dropping it is worth recording because it is the one disagreement the sender check below cannot catch. That check compares the sender's coin selection against the sender's own reading of the scriptSigs, so all it establishes is that the sender agrees with itself. A receiver applying BIP352's rule to an uncompressed input derives a different A, finds nothing, and the payment is unspendable with every check passing.

The cost of conforming is that 25399862 of 553844021 recovered inputs, 4.6 percent all-time, cannot be spent directly into a silent payment. That share is falling: the era sample puts uncompressed at 16.4 percent of 2014 spends and under 2 percent since, and the holder of such a coin can consolidate it into a compressed output first. One extra transaction is a smaller price than an interoperability hazard carried by every wallet built from this document.

What this does not establish: it measures scriptSigs that exist, so it says nothing about a wallet that starts emitting a shape nobody has used yet. The argument above covers that case anyway, since it rests on what the interpreter will accept rather than on what has been seen.

A receiver leaves out an input it cannot read a compressed key from and keeps the rest of the transaction, which is BIP352's rule and is pinned by its vector for uncompressed keys. Aborting instead would drop a transaction the BIP keeps, so a sender following the BIP would pay an output such a receiver never looks for. Once only compressed keys count, the inputs a reader fails on are exactly the uncompressed ones, so there is no separate class of unreadable input left for an abort to protect against.

== Why labels cost more ==

BIP352 lets a receiver hand out several addresses that all pay the same wallet, by tweaking the spend key with a label. One of them, label 0, is reserved for the receiver's own change.

The adaptation changes what they cost. In BIP352 a receiver recovers a label by subtracting the unlabelled candidate from the output key, which the transaction publishes, so any number of labels costs the same to scan. Paying to a hash instead means the transaction reveals only HASH160 of the output key and there is nothing to subtract, so labels have to be walked forward, on every transaction scanned. Scanning cost is linear in the number of labels a receiver holds, where in BIP352 it is flat.

What a label costs is a curve operation and not a hash. The implementation parses the candidate back from its 33 bytes and multiplies the generator by the label tweak again for every label on every transaction, measured at 26 microseconds a label a transaction. The tweak times the generator is the same point every time, so holding each label's point and adding it would leave one point addition and one HASH160 a label, which is what this section should be able to say. That change has not been made, because it is a cost rather than a defect and it sits in the part of the code the vectors pin.

So labels work, and they are not free the way they are in the BIP. A receiver that wants one address per payer should expect its scanning cost to scale with the number of payers. The reference implementation records every label it hands out, because the address derives from the seed and the label number and therefore looks correct whether or not anything remembered it, while a label the scanner does not know about is a payment nobody ever sees.

== The rule a sender must follow ==

The sender and the receiver derive the input set from different material. The sender knows which coins it owns. The receiver recovers the pubkeys from the scriptSigs, which do not exist until signing has finished. Those are two implementations of one rule, and when they disagree the output commits to a key neither party holds and the coins are simply gone. Writing the rule carefully twice is not sufficient, because the second copy is free to drift and because a signer can emit a scriptSig shape the reader interprets differently.

So a conforming sender must not merely select the right inputs, it must verify agreement before broadcasting. After signing, rerun the eligibility rule over the final scriptSigs and require that the keys it recovers sum to the same A the outputs were derived from, refusing to broadcast otherwise. Stated over public keys like that, rather than by rebuilding the output from private keys as the reference implementation happens to do, it is the same check and a signer that keeps its keys off the host can perform it. This costs one addition per input and it turns a disagreement into a failed send with the coins still unspent.

What it does not do is catch a receiver whose rule is stricter than the sender's. The check establishes that the sender agrees with itself, so it closes the case where a signer rewrites a scriptSig into a shape the sender's own reader would miss, and nothing more. The compressed-key departure above is exactly the case it cannot see, which is why that departure has to be settled rather than left to the check.

This protects a sender against its own bugs. It does nothing about a third-party wallet that implements this scheme and gets the rule wrong, which is the argument for stating the check here rather than leaving it as an implementation detail.

== Security considerations ==

The shared secret multiply runs on a secret scalar, <code>input_hash * a</code> when sending and <code>input_hash * b_scan</code> when receiving. Leaking b_scan exposes every payment ever made to that address, which is worse than leaking a spending key for a single output. The reference implementation uses <code>secp256k1_ecdh</code>, which goes through <code>secp256k1_ecmult_const</code>, and not <code>secp256k1_ec_pubkey_tweak_mul</code>, which reaches the variable time <code>secp256k1_ecmult</code> meant for verification. Anyone reimplementing this should check which one they are calling, because both compile and both produce the right answer.

A receiver that publishes one address forever hands every payer the same string, so payers can establish between themselves that they paid the same party. The sender always knows which output it created, so the scheme hides the link from third parties and not from the counterparty. The link also returns on chain when the receiver spends: a tip jar that sweeps its tips in one transaction shows every tipper the whole cluster, and each of them already knows which output was theirs. That is the use the motivation leads with, so it belongs here rather than being left implicit.

A serving peer is trusted to be complete. <code>sptweaks</code> names the block its entries were derived from, and a node that cannot answer for that block says nothing rather than answering for another, so a client can reject an answer that does not match its own header at that height. A client must perform that check; the message being right is not the same as the client verifying it.

What remains is omission. Nothing commits a block's tweaks, so a peer that leaves one entry out hides one payment and the client cannot tell. The next section sketches what closing that would take.

Scanning needs the wallet unlocked. The keys are derived from the HD master on every call and the live scanner returns early while locked, so an encrypted wallet receives nothing until it is opened, and the scan key is never held apart from the spend key. A light client that wants to scan with the scan key alone, which is the separation the scheme is built around, cannot do it with this wallet as written.

== Committing to a block's tweaks ==

This section is a sketch for discussion rather than a specification, and nothing in it is implemented.

The attack is one peer omitting one entry. The client derives no candidate for that transaction, so it never asks the filter about it, so it never fetches the block, and the payment is invisible to it. A filter false positive costs a download and is self-correcting. An omission costs a payment and is permanent, and the client cannot detect it from what it holds, because a block's tweak set is not derivable from its header.

A tweak header chain is the cheap answer and it is not a commitment. Hashing each block's entries into a running chain, the way BIP157 does for filters, lets a client compare one peer's answer against another's and see that they disagree. It does not say which one is lying, and a client whose peers are all one party learns nothing. It raises the cost of the attack from one peer to all of a client's peers, which is worth having, and it should not be described as making the answer verifiable.

What makes the answer verifiable is a consensus commitment. A block's tweak set is a deterministic function of the block and the coins it spends, so any node holding the chain can recompute it and needs no commitment at all. The parties that need one are exactly the clients that do not hold the chain. Putting a root of the tweak set in the coinbase, as the segwit commitment is, makes it checkable under SPV: the client verifies the root against the coinbase transaction and the coinbase against the merkle root in the header it already has. That is a soft fork, and it asks miners to change coinbase construction, which on this chain is the hardest thing in the list to move. Dogecoin's coinbase already carries the merged mining commitment, so whether a second one can sit beside it without disturbing existing pool software is the first question to answer and is not one this document has investigated.

There is a weaker measure available now that needs no consensus change and no new message. A client that is fetching a block anyway, because the filter matched, can also ask for the prevouts of one transaction it did not expect to care about and recompute that block's tweaks itself. Sampling at a chosen rate catches a peer that omits systematically, at a bandwidth cost proportional to the rate. It does not catch a single omission aimed at a single payment, which is the case that actually loses money, so it is a way to find a bad peer rather than a way to trust a good one.

The recommendation is to build the header chain, because it is cheap and composes with asking several peers, and to state the limit plainly in the wallet rather than let completeness be assumed. A client scanning from one peer is trusting that peer not to hide a payment from it, and that remains true until there is a commitment in the chain.

== Adoption is the most likely thing to kill it ==

A new address format is worth nothing until exchanges and wallets accept it. Dogecoin's wallet ecosystem is small and format changes have historically moved slowly. If Core ships this and nothing else does, the result is a correct feature almost nobody can receive on, and that is the likeliest failure mode here by a wide margin. It is worth deciding whether there is appetite from the larger services before this goes any further, because the integration work on their side is considerably larger than the wallet work on ours.

== Backwards compatibility ==

There is no consensus change. The outputs are ordinary P2PKH, so every existing node validates and relays them exactly as it does any other payment, and no node needs to know this scheme exists.

The address itself is a new string and is the whole compatibility surface. It is bech32m and does not begin with D, so any service that validates recipients by pattern matching on a leading D will reject it. Those services need a change before a user can paste one in, which is the same class of work SegWit addresses required elsewhere and is the reason the adoption section above is not a formality. The prefix is <code>dsp</code> on mainnet and <code>tdsp</code> on testnet, which does begin with a lowercase d, so a service matching a leading D case-insensitively reaches the length check rather than failing on the first character.

The layout inside that string is BIP352's exactly: the version is one bech32 data character ahead of the 66 key bytes, as a segwit address carries its witness version, rather than a byte converted along with the keys. Only the prefix is Dogecoin's, so the version character and the payload of an address built from BIP352's own test vector keys come out identical to the published <code>sp1</code> address, and the unit test asserts that against the spec's string rather than against what it just encoded. Versions 1 to 30 decode as payable by reading the first 66 bytes and discarding the rest, and only version 31 is refused, which is what BIP352 requires of a version 0 sender. An earlier draft of this document recorded the opposite on all three counts, because the branch then packed the version as a byte and rejected every version it did not know.

A wallet that does not implement this is unaffected and simply cannot send to or receive on a silent payment address. The tweak index is optional and off by default, so a node that does not set it carries no extra cost.

== Reference implementation ==

[https://github.com/xanimo/dogecoin/tree/c31cdfda581a9749453324d90b0ea4413eff4486 sp-on-filters]
on xanimo/dogecoin carries the bech32 and bech32m codec, <code>src/bip352.{h,cpp}</code> with the ported BIP352 test vectors and the constant time ECDH, <code>src/index/tweakindex.{h,cpp}</code> on the background index framework, silent payment keys derived from the HD seed, the three RPCs <code>getsilentpaymentaddress</code>, <code>sendtosilentpaymentaddress</code> and <code>scansilentpayments</code>, live scanning under <code>-silentpaymentscan</code>, labels that survive a restart, and end to end functional tests.

The light client half is there too, in three parts. A serving node answers <code>getsptweaks</code> with a block's tweaks, behind <code>NODE_SP_TWEAKS</code> and <code>-peersptweaks</code>. <code>GetCandidateScripts</code> builds the scripts a receiver would own from a tweak alone, which is what lets a client ask about a block it has not downloaded. <code>scansilentpaymentsfiltered</code> chains the two: it derives the candidates for each block's tweaks, asks that block's BIP158 filter whether any appear, and reads only the blocks that match. Over a regtest chain of 228 blocks where 26 carry an eligible transaction it reads 2, querying 52 candidate scripts with no false positives. That ratio is not a mainnet figure and should not be read as one, since regtest is far sparser than the real chain and the wallet held no labels. The false positive rate does not need measuring at all: the filter keeps M = 784931 and the client asks one script per tweak plus one per label held, so the chance of reading a block for nothing is about tweaks * (labels + 1) / 784931. At the mean block and no labels that is 0.011 percent; for a thousand-tweak block and a wallet holding a hundred labels it is 13 percent, which is the cost the labels section warns about showing up again on the client side. It is stacked on the BIP157 and BIP158 compact filter backport, which is unmerged and proposed as [https://github.com/dogecoin/dogecoin/pull/4031 dogecoin/dogecoin#4031].
[https://github.com/xanimo/dogecoin/tree/394374e4c15319571eb14a3f3019f7e807249c67 feature/bip352-silent-payments]
is the same feature without that dependency, where the index is driven from ConnectBlock and does need a reindex to populate. It predates the rule as stated here: it matches P2PKH through <code>Solver</code> and accepts both key lengths, so it is linked as the standalone variant of the design rather than as an implementation of this text. Bringing it to the same rule, or dropping the link, is one of the conditions below.

The eligibility rule has been run over the whole chain rather than a sample, which is where the numbers above come from, and the filter arithmetic has been measured against the filters a mainnet node serves.

The tweak index was built over mainnet once, before the rule was narrowed, and that build is no longer current. Rebuilding it from genesis on the same datadir stops at height 1111, where the block index records an undo position two bytes short of where that record begins. Everything checked says the datadir rather than the code is at fault: the undo file is internally consistent and each record verifies against its own checksum, the block is a coinbase-only one whose undo payload is the single byte an empty record serializes to, the block filter index on that datadir holds an entry for the same block and so performed the same read successfully when it was built, and the three callers of the undo read in the tree pass identical arguments. Repairing it means a reindex of the datadir, which has not been run. So the index is known to build and serve on regtest and the whole-chain build is currently unreproducible on the hardware to hand.

Not done: the light client has never run against mainnet, only regtest. The filter false positive rate it depends on is measured above against real mainnet filters, but the <code>getsptweaks</code> round trip is not, because serving tweaks needs the index. No wallet outside Core implements either side.

Review state: one reviewer has built the branch from source over three rounds, run the unit and functional suites, built it under the address and undefined behaviour sanitisers, and written independent regressions including a differential run that derives every tweak and every output a second way and compares. No round has come back clean, and the third found more than the second.

The first round found two defects. Enabling live scanning skipped the payment in the next block, recoverable only by an explicit scan. The other was not in this feature and is the more useful finding: the shared background index persisted its progress locator before writing the block it named, so a crash in that window left the block unindexed and the restart resumed past it. That is <code>BaseIndex</code>, which <code>txindex</code> and the block filter index use equally, and it is fixed on the compact filter branch rather than here.

The second round found that a request made behind a one-block reorg was answered with the new block's hash beside the old block's entries. The index now records the block each height's entries were computed from and answers only for that block. That binding has no regression test: the race needs the window between a reorg and the index following it, and a functional test that waits for the index closes the window.

The third round found seven, three of them losing track of money: a wallet encrypted after giving its address out scanned for a different address and found nothing paid to the published one; a scan over history credited outputs that had since been spent; and <code>-salvagewallet</code> dropped the label records, so payments to a labelled address stopped being found. The rest were a live scan holding <code>cs_main</code> while it caught up, a <code>getsptweaks</code> answer held in memory whole so that a 60-byte request could hold megabytes per connection, <code>-silentpaymentscan</code> accepted without <code>-tweakindex</code> and silently scanning nothing, and a unit case reading off the stack. All seven are fixed in the linked tree, six by the reviewer's patches and the last independently.

Nobody has read the cryptography who works on secp256k1.

== What this document does not yet specify ==

This is a rationale and a measurement report. Someone building a second wallet from it would have to read the branch for all of the following, and each is a place two implementations would silently disagree.

The key derivation. Scan and spend sit at m/352'/3'/0'/0' and m/352'/3'/0'/1', where BIP352 puts them elsewhere, so a seed carried to a wallet following the BIP finds nothing.

The sighash rule. BIP352 states that ANYONECANPAY is unsafe, because the inputs must not change once the sender has signed. This document says nothing about it.

The output group limit, which the branch fixes at 2323, and the <code>getsptweaks</code> and <code>sptweaks</code> payload layouts, which exist only as code.

What a wallet owes an address it gave out before <code>encryptwallet</code>. Encryption replaces the HD seed, so the address changes, and a payment to the published one is derivable only from the seed the wallet had before. The linked tree records the seed each address was given out under and scans against that seed as well as the one in use, which keeps the payments coming; a wallet encrypted before that change holds no such record and needs the key <code>dumpwallet</code> marks <code>inactivehdmaster=1</code>. Whether the address should change at all on encryption is a design question this document does not settle.

That an answer to <code>getsptweaks</code> may stop short. The linked tree sends one block at a time and stops once the peer's send buffer is over its limit, so a client that reads slowly gets part of its range and has to ask again from the first block it did not receive. Each message names its height and its block, so nothing more is needed to do that, but a short answer must not be read as no payments in the rest of the range.

== What has to be true before this leaves Draft ==

This is wallet level, so nothing here can split the chain. What it can do is lose a payment: derive an output neither side holds the key to, or one the receiver's scan never finds. The conditions below are about two implementations agreeing and about this document being enough to build the second one from. Each is written so that it can be checked, and none of them is a date.

The rule agrees with BIP352 on every input. A reader leaves out an input that reveals no compressed key and keeps the transaction, a sender leaves that input's key out of its sum, and the open question on abort or skip is closed in this text the same way. By the argument under the eligibility rule a P2PKH spend always pushes its key, so once only compressed keys count, the inputs a reader cannot read are exactly the uncompressed ones, and aborting on them drops a transaction BIP352 keeps. This holds when BIP352's vector for uncompressed keys passes as a unit case, when the vector generator reproduces the committed file, and when a payment from one compressed and one uncompressed coin, built to the BIP's rule outside the wallet, is served by the index and found by the wallet's scan.

Nothing is left to the branch. Every item under "What this document does not yet specify" is either specified here, with vectors, or stated to be out of scope: the derivation paths, the sighash rule, the output group limit, and the byte layouts of <code>getsptweaks</code> and <code>sptweaks</code>. The address encoding has come off that list, since it now follows BIP352's layout and is pinned to the spec's own published address rather than to the branch. This holds when someone who has not read the branch builds a sender and a scanner from this document and reproduces every vector.

The tweak service answers for the block it names. An <code>sptweaks</code> message carries the hash of the block its entries were derived from, a serving node that cannot answer for that block says nothing, and this document describes the message as built, including what a client has to check. In review a request made right behind a one-block reorg was answered with the new block's hash and the old block's entries, twenty times in twenty, and the only test of the hash compared the node with itself. The test now asks across a reorg, takes its headers from a second node rather than from the node being tested, requires every entry to index a transaction the named block actually has, and refuses any answer naming a block the reorg discarded. What it does not reach is the race itself: removing the hash binding does not fail it, because the disconnected heights are erased on rewind and waiting for the index to catch up closes the window. This holds when that binding fails a test with the binding removed, which needs the index driven directly rather than through a node.

The numbers are of the rule as specified. The corpus harness has been re-run with compressed keys only and every figure above is from that run, and the filter false positive rate has been measured against mainnet filters. What is left is the index rebuilt over mainnet in its final layout and the <code>getsptweaks</code> round trip run against mainnet once, both of which need a datadir whose undo bookkeeping is intact.

The links are the code. The reference implementation links point at commits that implement the rule this text states, so <code>feature/bip352-silent-payments</code> is either brought to the same rule or no longer linked.

Four more before the implementation is put forward for a release.

A second implementation exists. A wallet that shares no code with the reference branch pays an address the branch generated and is paid by it, with and without labels. The loss this scheme can cause is a disagreement between two implementations, and with one implementation that has never been tested.

The parsers have been fuzzed. The address decoder, the key extraction from a scriptSig and the <code>getsptweaks</code> handler each have a fuzz target that has run under the sanitisers, and the key extraction is compared, over consensus-valid spends, with the search BIP352's reference code performs.

Someone else has read the cryptography. A reviewer other than the author, who works with secp256k1, has read the ECDH path, the tagged hashes and the label derivation, including what changes when the output is a hash and not a key, and has said so in public.

The compact filter backport this is stacked on has merged, since review of this branch cannot finish before review of that one.

None of this answers the adoption section, which is a separate gate and not a security one.

== Open questions for discussion ==

Whether to extend past P2PKH to P2SH, and which redeem script shapes would qualify.

Whether the tweak index ships in Core at all, given that the people who most need it are the people who cannot run a full node.

What the address prefix should be, and whether a bech32m string is acceptable to services that currently pattern match on a leading D. The implementation takes <code>dsp</code> and <code>tdsp</code>, which are not registered anywhere and are only what the branch uses.

Which of the measures in the tweak commitment section to build, and whether a coinbase commitment is worth a soft fork for a wallet feature at all.

What service bit and message names the tweak service should use. The implementation takes <code>1 << 25</code> for <code>NODE_SP_TWEAKS</code> and the names <code>getsptweaks</code> and <code>sptweaks</code>, chosen high to stay clear of upstream assignments rather than by any authority. These are wire-visible and changing them after anything ships is a compatibility break, so they want settling here.

